  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;reaction decreases with increase in steric crowding.&nbsp;In the given set of molecules the second molecule(Tertiary alkyl halide)&nbsp;is sterically crowded, hence, it is correct option. More stable carbocation formed, higher will be the reactivity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math>. Benzyl carbocation is more reactive than aliphatic carbocation.</p><p>The deactivating groups decreases the rate of electrophilic substitution reaction.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is strong deactivating group for electrophilic substitution.</p><p>In the aromatic nucleophilic substitution reactions, the intermediate formed is carbanion and, it is stabilised by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;at ortho or para positions.</p><p>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math> all are correct.</p></blockquote>",
